# {{content_id}} cross-runner reproduction

Title: {{display_name}}
Content id: {{content_id}}
Cell: {{cell_label}}
Checkpoint: {{checkpoint_kind}}

The two observation JSONs in `{{observation_dir}}cellgov/` and
`{{observation_dir}}rpcs3/` were produced by the runner-specific
capture flows below. The `compare_report.txt` and
`cross_runner_summary.json` next to this file are byte-deterministic
outputs of `cellgov dev fixture-gen` over those JSONs.

Both sides run the firmware this cell names. `--rpcs3-dir` on the
capture conversion below reads the version the other runner had
installed at that moment, so the version recorded is the one the dump
actually saw rather than whatever is installed whenever this report is
generated. A pair that disagrees is refused rather than rendered as a
parity verdict, and a capture converted without that flag is refused
for naming no version at all.

## Capture the CellGov observation

```bash
cargo run --release -p cellgov_cli -- boot run \
  --title-manifest title_manifests/{{content_id}}.toml {{selection_flags}} \
  --max-steps <N> \
  --save-observation tests/fixtures/{{content_id}}/cellgov/observation.json \
  --observation-manifest tests/fixtures/{{content_id}}/checkpoint.toml
```

The boot anchor is not captured here: it is keyed by the cell the run
composed and `cellgov dev record-anchors` is its only writer.

`--max-steps` is the instruction budget and defaults to 100,000.
That default halts well before a title reaches its checkpoint;
seeing the full breadth of boot currently needs a budget in the
1,000,000+ range. Raise `<N>` until the run reaches its
checkpoint (or settles at a deterministic wall) rather than
stopping at `MaxSteps`.

## Capture the RPCS3 observation

See [bridges/rpcs3-patch/README.md]({{repo_root_rel}}bridges/rpcs3-patch/README.md)
for the patched-runner build instructions. Install the firmware this
cell names into the runner before capturing, or the summary below is
refused; then:

```bash
# Dump filename encodes the checkpoint plus a <tag> the capturer
# fills in (e.g. "baseline", "with-patch0003", "rpcs3-0.0.40").
# Multiple dumps can co-exist for diffing against each other or
# against CellGov; the rpcs3/ directory's *.dump files are
# gitignored.
DUMP=tests/fixtures/{{content_id}}/rpcs3/dump_{{checkpoint_kind}}_<tag>.dump

CELLGOV_DUMP_PATH="$DUMP" \
CELLGOV_DUMP_REGIONS=<addr:size,...> \
  ./tools/rpcs3/rpcs3.exe --headless <eboot>

cargo run --release -p rpcs3_to_observation -- \
  --dump "$DUMP" \
  --manifest tests/fixtures/{{content_id}}/checkpoint.toml \
  --outcome <outcome> \
  --rpcs3-dir <runner-install-dir> \
  --output tests/fixtures/{{content_id}}/rpcs3/observation.json
```

The dump's first 4 bytes are `7f 45 4c 46` (ELF magic): the
checkpoint manifest's first region is the title's code segment,
which starts with the loaded ELF header. A dump that does not
start with `\x7fELF` signals either a manifest reorder, a
write-order bug in the dump-hook, or guest-memory corruption at
the code-region base.

## Regenerate the cross-runner report

```bash
cargo run --release -p cellgov_cli -- dev fixture-gen \
  --manifest title_manifests/{{content_id}}.toml {{selection_flags}} \
  --cellgov tests/fixtures/{{content_id}}/cellgov/observation.json \
  --rpcs3 tests/fixtures/{{content_id}}/rpcs3/observation.json
```

The triple lands in `{{cell_dir}}/`, the directory holding this file.

